/-
Johnson/G/PricedKeyLit.lean: the three priced plans of a key file at the full size, as written
values, for statements that Lean's kernel is to evaluate. Data alone: definitions without a
function; no theorem.

A priced plan (`OnBytes.Params.Planned`) is a setting and a list of rounds, each with its entry in
the parameter bytes and what the plan's verdict reads: an upper bound on the round's error as an
exact binary number, the group from which it was priced, the kind of the error, the footing of its
count, who pays for its work, and whether the word on its pricing contradicts the round. The planner
computes the plans from public files by parsing, which Lean's kernel does not evaluate. Here they
stand written out: for each chain the distinct rounds once, and the chain's list as runs "how often,
which record".
Main names:
  `pricedKeySetFull`: the setting; the three plans have one.
  `pricedKeyRecsFull0`, `1`, `2`: the distinct rounds of chain 0, 1, 2: 37, 48, 21 records. Two
    rounds are one record only if all seven fields are the same. What two chains share stands
    twice.
  `pricedKeyRunsFull0`, `1`, `2`: the runs: 4685, 48, 21 runs for 18852, 68, 30 rounds;
    neighbours of one record are merged. Chain 0's list is written in three parts.
Whence the numbers: a program wrote this file from a print that Lean's evaluator made, field by
field, of `m.p.chains.made.key`, m being what `Johnson.G.OfPlan.madeOf` returns at three public
files policy.txt, layout.txt and KL.bin of the full size, with the sums
  policy.txt ce9e126bb3ba7179319a31981a3fc36ca505d738348971f379dfc795f94a3fae
  KL.bin     1c3191164c5113fd04d5108a469d7b3d17f4ccb3256e8a676d15bdc94435b919
  layout.txt a99b964bbd3648d6e3b4007fc9370b5c70c7e9668848daa039820bc9e03e8f80.
No other file was read.
Limits: this file by itself holds the written values against nothing. A test by evaluation has held
them against the planner's own plans, field by field, at these three files; no test that a build
runs does. CHAIN 0's PLAN BEGINS WITH AN ENTRY UNDER THE NAME "RECORD" (the table's own sample): so
it has one round more than the chain's declared rounds. No round has `wordFault`. That an error
bounds what its name says is claimed nowhere here. Nothing on soundness.

-/
